she turns her experiences into music  into notes even the protests in her home  country turkey  these days composer zaineb ki disliolu  lives and works in berlin  the kreutzberg district of berlin  noisy chaotic full of contradictions the  perfect place to work  it helps me focus once i even finished  composing one of my piano pieces called  divant etlang in the airport  her most recent work is called durak it  was commissioned by deutsche welle for  this year's beethoven festival in bonn  her compositions are known for their  precision and uncompromising attention  to detail  is at home in the international new  music scene she was born in izmir  studied in istanbul zarb looking  strasbourg and carls lua and spent a  year doing research in paris  i can't even really imagine living in a  place where i'd say this is where i'll  stay where i belong  and for me being rootless is something  good useful productive  when zainebkidisliolu left turkey 12  years ago her piano stayed in istanbul  she taught herself how to compose  without an instrument she does her best  work in public places like cafes and  libraries  and her music changed too as a result it  grew more complex bolder and more  experimental  all i need is myself  my mind which gives me the imagination i  need to create my works this is a handy  forced and can  and  a pencil  some paper  and it helps to have an eraser  shortly after she began working on the  commission last may the people of  istanbul took to the streets they  demanded more freedom more democracy and  self-determination the police responded  to the demonstrators with brutality and  force  suddenly the place she'd left behind  turkey was calling to her  but what was i supposed to do  just quit what i was doing buy a gas  mask and go to istanbul and be useful  nuts  the title of her new work durak has  several meanings in turkish a break a  decisive step a phase a word that  captures the dichotomies of her work  this is my natural this is my language  music  and that was the decision i faced  will i take to the streets  or will i choose a more solitary slow  path of trying to find something to say  music is her language but that path too  didn't happen overnight  kadisliolu's parents are both artists  but not musicians her mother's an  actress and theater director her  stepfather is a painter  when she was eight zainab discovered her  parents record collection rock music  nothing classical but it captivated her  like nothing else  music can transform your entire life  it's a kind of vitality that you  suddenly discover and feel within  yourself  you didn't even realize it and then  there it is  it can be a moment an experience  and all of a sudden your entire world  has changed  unlike many other composers zaineb  kudisliolu doesn't use a computer or  simulation software to create her work  she doesn't actually hear her finished  piece until it's time for rehearsal  that's another thing i love about my  career  it's part of what makes it so exciting  the first rehearsal is really a magical  experience  she composed durak for the students of  the istanbul university orchestra it's  the first time the young musicians are  performing contemporary classical music  come on  it's a demanding work for the performers  and the audience  but the premier is a success the turkish  ambassador is a bit more restrained in  his applause he's less than pleased  about the political implications of the  work  durak also represents a break a rupture  for the composer herself she's a musical  nomad but now turkey her homeland and  the protest there have taken up  residence in her work the result is a  powerful statement set to the language  of music  you
 

